WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.280
A gaming PC becomes a capable AI workstation through deliberate configuration,

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.320
not through a single installation or expensive upgrade.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.640
Begin with an exact inventory of the GPU, VRAM, CPU, system RAM, storage, motherboard,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.160
power supply, and cooling.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.240
Those facts establish the machine's operating envelope and prevent model or hardware choices based on assumptions.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.940
Select an operating system that you can maintain and that supports the intended acceleration path.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:31.260
Treat GPU drivers, CUDA, ROCm, Vulkan, and the selected runtime as a compatibility chain.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.580
Prove that acceleration is active with a simple setup before adding interfaces and orchestration.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.620
Then choose models by workload and available memory.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.040
Quantization, context length, and partial offload are engineering tradeoffs,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.700
not magic ways to erase resource limits.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.840
Measure real tasks.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.020
Generation speed, prompt processing, GPU utilization, VRAM, system RAM, temperature, power,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.680
and noise together describe the user experience.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.860
Change one variable at a time and preserve a known-good baseline.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.420
Upgrade only when evidence identifies a clear bottleneck and the proposed part

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.700
can be supported by the case,

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.980
motherboard, power supply, and cooling system.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.500
Finally, protect the workstation.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.920
Use trusted model and software sources, restrict API exposure,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.100
control sensitive prompts and logs, patch deliberately, and keep configuration backups.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:22.180
The goal is not to run the biggest available model or assemble the most complicated stack.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.740
The goal is reliable, private local AI that serves a defined workload,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.300
remains understandable to its operator, and can be recovered when something changes.
